1 NAME José Julián /Flores Hinojosa/
2 GIVN José Julián
2 SURN Flores Hinojosa
2 _MARNM Flores Hinojosa
2 NICK Julian Flores
2 NICK Jose Julian Flores
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 21 AUG 1744
2 PLAC Mier, Tamaulipas, México
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 10 FEB 1835
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Many websites state that Julian's name is Jose Julian Flores but the "Jose" is not on the 1750 census nor his marriage records.
2 CONT
2 CONT Julian and his son, Ventura, received each the "San Diego de Arriba" and the "San Diego de Abajo" land grants respectfully from the Spanish crown when Spain ruled Mexico. Records indicate that after the Mexican War of Independence
2 CONC , Julian and Ventura paid the new Mexican government a tax to keep the properties.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other docs indicate that Julian died intestate with 6 surviving children who each received 1/6 of his land.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1750 Camargo Census records show Julian at 6 years old so born in 1744 or 1743
2 CONT
2 CONT The Texas State Historical Association website states "Around 1800 San Diego de Arriba and San Diego de Abajo, two grants totaling eight leagues of land, were granted by the Spanish government to Julián Flores and his son Ventura.
2 CONC  The grants were surveyed in 1806 by José Faustino Contreras, the surveyor general of San Luis Potosí. Julián and Ventura Flores arrived in 1809 and received their deed in 1812. The first settlers may have been Julián Flores's her
2 CONC dsmen, who had settled on his ranch there by 1815; four years later the Flores family authorized an agent to found a town "at the place called San Diego.""
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT My grandmother Maryita Garcia claimed that Julian's parents are Luciano Flores and Patty McGovern but Julian's marriage records in Mier state that his parents are Jose Diego Flores and Maria Luisa Hinojosa.  A handwritten document
2 CONC  written by Capt. Jack Ross Everrett as a family bible record states Julian's name as Julian Flores McGovern.  Jack Everett, grandson of John and Sarah (Fagan) Everett, married Julian's great granddaughter, Antonio Flores.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many websites state that Julian's name is Jose Julian Flores but the "Jose" is not on the 1750 census nor his marriage records.
2 CONT
2 CONT Julian and his son, Ventura, received each the "San Diego de Arriba" and the "San Diego de Abajo" land grants respectfully from the Spanish crown when Spain ruled Mexico.  Records indicate that after the Mexican War of Independenc
2 CONC e, Julian and Ventura paid the new Mexican government a tax to keep the properties.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Other docs indicate that Julian died intestate with 6 surviving children who each received 1/6 of his land.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1750 Camargo Census records show Julian at 6 years old so born in 1744 or 1743
2 CONT
2 CONT The Texas State Historical Association website states "Around 1800 San Diego de Arriba and San Diego de Abajo, two grants totaling eight leagues of land, were granted by the Spanish government to Julián Flores and his son Ventura.
2 CONC  The grants were surveyed in 1806 by José Faustino Contreras, the surveyor general of San Luis Potosí. Julián and Ventura Flores arrived in 1809 and received their deed in 1812. The first settlers may have been Julián Flores's her
2 CONC dsmen, who had settled on his ranch there by 1815; four years later the Flores family authorized an agent to found a town "at the place called San Diego.""
