1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ABSOLOM LINE--Juan Angel Saenz and his wife Ana Dominga Garza. He was an original land grantee from the Spanish Land Grants of 1767. Part of the land he owned is today's Roma, Texas. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT From Raul N. Longoria's Genealogy Database:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.raullongoria.net/Genealogy/FamilyTree/b200.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan Angel SAENZ [1897] 
2 CONT *died on 29 November 1802. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan Angel Saens purchased Porciones Nos. 70, 71 and 72, in Mier, and also Porciones Nos. 109 and 110 in Camargo. Today, the town of Roma, Texas is situated in Porciones Nos. 71 and 72.
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: Ana Dominga de la GARZA. Ana Dominga de la GARZA and Juan Angel SAENZ were married on 11 May 1757. Children were: 
2 CONT *1. Carmen SAENZ, 
2 CONT *2. Leocadia SAENZ, 
2 CONT *3. Josefa SAENZ GARZA.
2 CONT
2 CONT Footnote:
2 CONT *1897. Family Tree Book (The F. W. Seabury Papers), a transcription of the original papers in The Center for American History at The University of Texas at Austin, transcribed and edited by Joel Rene Escobar, New Santander Press, 
2 CONC Edinburg, TX, 1995, Page 70.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Texas State Historical Association:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/online/articles/hgr06
2 CONT
2 CONT Corrales de Saenz was founded in the mid-1760s by local ranchers Miguel, Gerónimo, and Juan Ángel Saenz, followers of José de Escandón from the Spanish colonial city of Mier, and it is possible that what came to be known as Roma-L
2 CONC os Saenz and Ciudad Miguel Alemán were originally part of the same city, San Pedro de Roma, Tamaulipas. The area was known as Buena Vista and then Garcia Ranch. In 1848, when it became a part of the United States, the name was cha
2 CONC nged to Roma, suggested by the Oblates of Mary Immaculate, who founded a mission there in the mid-1850s.
