1 NOTE {geni:about_me} .Rafael Garcia refused to give information on his background to Seabury during his genealogical interviews. I've come to believe that he didn't want to reveal his parentage due to the close interpersonal relationsh
2 CONC ip with the Moreno family. I could not locate any records on his family until recently which confirmed the well know genealogy that was provided without citation before.The story of his family is told in a parable El Mesquite, wri
2 CONC tten by his descendant ElenaZamora O'Shea. This parable is a loving remembrance of the lost culture of the ranching families of south Texas. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jose Maria Garcia Salinas was the Original Grantee of the Laureles, a 4 league tract in what is now Nueces County. He was the brother of Rafael Garcia Salinas, Original Grantee of the La Mestena y Gonzalena 5-league tract in Brook
2 CONC s County.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.raullongoria.net/Genealogy/FamilyTree/b94.html#P1409
2 CONT
2 CONT Rafael GARCIA SALINAS [1873],[1957] was the Original Grantee of in La Mestena y Gonzalena, Brooks County, TX. Rafael Garcia Salinas was the Original Grantee of La Mestena y Gonzalena, a 5 league tract in what is now Brooks County,
2 CONC  Texas. He was the brother of Jose Maria Garcia Salinas, Original Grantee of the Laureles 4-league tract in Nueces County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some people claimed that Rafael Garcia Salinas was really the owner of the La Mestena tract granted to Rafael Garcia Saenz as well as the La Mestena tract granted to Ysidro Garcia Saenz. Since they were his sons, it is easy to see
2 CONC  why people would make that claim. The 3 tracts were side by side and together comprised 15 leagues.
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: Josefa SAENZ GARZA. Josefa SAENZ GARZA and Rafael GARCIA SALINAS were married. Children were: 
2 CONT *1. Jose Maria GARCIA, 
2 CONT *2. Justo GARCIA, 
2 CONT *3. Lorenzo GARCIA, 
2 CONT *4. Ysidro GARCIA SAENZ, 
2 CONT *5. Rafael GARCIA SAENZ, 
2 CONT *6. Maria Concepcion GARCIA, 
2 CONT *7. Ruperta GARCIA, 
2 CONT *8. Basilia GARCIA.
2 CONT
2 CONT Footnotes:
2 CONT *1873. Family Tree Book (The F. W. Seabury Papers), a transcription of the original papers in The Center for American History at The University of Texas at Austin, transcribed and edited by Joel Rene Escobar, New Santander Press, 
2 CONC Edinburg, TX, 1995, Page 74.
2 CONT *1897. Family Tree Book (The F. W. Seabury Papers), a transcription of the original papers in The Center for American History at The University of Texas at Austin, transcribed and edited by Joel Rene Escobar, New Santander Press, 
2 CONC Edinburg, TX, 1995, Page 70.
2 CONT *1957. Family Tree Book (The F. W. Seabury Papers), a transcription of the original papers in The Center for American History at The University of Texas at Austin, transcribed and edited by Joel Rene Escobar, New Santander Press, 
2 CONC Edinburg, TX, 1995, Page 72.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Texas State Historical Association:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/online/articles/hcb16
2 CONT
2 CONT In the sixteenth century the Spanish made various explorations of the area (Brooks County, Texas); however, because of its distance from the coast, the lack of a major river, and wide stretches of deep sand that made travel diffic
2 CONC ult, the area remained unsettled. Although land grants in the Trans-Nueces region were made as early as 1767, it was not until the 1800s that an effort was made to introduce colonists into the territory that became Brooks County. 
2 CONC About twenty-five land grants were made in the Brooks County area by the Spanish and Mexican governments. The earliest, the San Salvador del Tule grant, was given to Juan José Ballí on November 8, 1797. Other important early grant
2 CONC s included El Encino en el Poso, made to Luciano Chapa around 1827, and El Paisano, made to Ramón de la Garza around the same time. But because of its isolation most of the families receiving grants settled along the Rio Grande ra
2 CONC ther than in the Brooks County area and only sporadically brought their cattle to the region.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between the Texas Revolution and the end of the Mexican War, Brooks County lay in the disputed territory between the Rio Grande and the Nueces River. During these conflicts many of the original grantees fled to Mexico, and much of
2 CONC  the area was occupied only by wandering vaqueros. Gradually, with the cessation of hostilities, some families returned, but frequent droughts and lack of transportation discouraged permanent settlement.
